Amazon CloudWatch Service Notes
Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service built for AWS resources and the applications you run on AWS. It provides data and actionable insights to monitor your applications, respond to system-wide performance changes, and optimize resource utilization.
1. Core Concepts & Terminology
•	Metrics: A fundamental concept in CloudWatch. A metric represents a time-ordered set of data points (e.g., CPU utilization of an EC2 instance, number of Lambda invocations, S3 bucket size).
o	Metrics resolution defaults to 1-minute intervals. Detailed Monitoring (additional cost for some services like EC2) enables 1-second intervals.
•	Namespaces: A container for metrics, allowing you to group metrics from different AWS services or applications (e.g., AWS/EC2, AWS/Lambda, or your custom application namespace).
•	Dimensions: Attributes used to uniquely identify a metric (e.g., InstanceId, FunctionName).
•	Alarms: Watches a single metric and performs a specified action (like sending an SNS notification, triggering an Auto Scaling action, or stopping an EC2 instance) when the metric reaches a threshold.
2. CloudWatch Logs
CloudWatch Logs is used to centralize and store log files from various AWS services and application sources.
•	Log Groups: Groups of log streams that share the same retention, monitoring, and access control settings.
•	Log Streams: A sequence of log events that share the same source (e.g., logs from a single EC2 instance or Lambda function invocation).
•	Log Retention: You can configure how long logs are stored (from 1 day to indefinitely).
•	Metrics Filters: You can create custom metrics based on patterns found within log data (e.g., counting the number of "ERROR" lines in application logs).
•	Log Insights: A powerful interactive query language used to search and analyze log data efficiently.
3. CloudWatch Events / Amazon EventBridge
EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications using data from your own applications, SaaS applications, and AWS services. It was previously called CloudWatch Events.
•	Event Bus: A pipeline for events.
•	Rules: Define criteria for routing events to specific targets.
•	Targets: The AWS resource that responds to the event (e.g., Lambda function, SQS queue, EC2 instance).
•	Schedules: EventBridge is used to run tasks on a schedule (similar to cron jobs), triggering targets like Lambda functions at specific times.
4. CloudWatch Dashboards
•	Customizable Visualization: Dashboards allow you to create reusable visualizations of your metrics and alarms in one place.
•	Cross-Service Visibility: You can combine data from different AWS services (EC2, RDS, Lambda) onto a single pane of glass for unified monitoring.
5. Billing and Pricing
•	CloudWatch provides basic monitoring for most services free of charge.
•	Detailed monitoring, custom metrics, and log ingestion/retention above the free tier generally incur costs based on usage volume.


